```console
$ useful workflow run show --help
Show a workflow run under the useful home or in Cloud

Usage: useful workflow run show [OPTIONS] <RUN>

Arguments:
  <RUN>  Run directory, run ID or subject

Options:
      --attempt <NUMBER>       Attempt number to show alone, of a run under the useful home
      --wait                   Observe the run until it settles
      --timeout <DURATION>     Stop waiting after a positive duration (units: ms, s, m, or h; a bare number means seconds)
      --org <ORG>              Organization ID or exact slug to act in (default: USEFUL_ORG, then the project of this checkout, then your only organization)
      --plain                  Force plain human presentation
      --json                   Print the workflow result as JSON
      --color <WHEN>           Select renderer color behavior [default: auto] [possible values: auto, always, never]
      --api-key-file <PATH|->  Authenticate with a service API key from a private file, or - for standard input
      --allow-insecure-http    Allow this command's Useful Machinery requests over insecure HTTP connections
  -h, --help                   Print help

RUN is a run directory, a run ID or a subject. An ID or a subject is looked up under the useful home and, when the home holds no such run, in Cloud in the organization in effect: an ID names the Cloud run of that ID, and a subject the newest run, for each definition, whose subject claim names it, among the organization's 500 newest live runs and then, when every live run was read, its 500 newest ended ones. A run under the home is read from its records without a request; a Cloud run is read with its definition, subject claim, workflow state and outcome, latest progress, exits, newest controls and newest trigger deliveries.

--attempt reads a run under the home; --wait and --timeout follow a Cloud run until it is final.

```
